From 056e752f1897f2650aa43ed7d756400386d7b933 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mjb-is <mjb-is@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 01:24:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 069/108] snapshot: delete our VSS shadow copies after every
 backup, through the VSS API

Releasing the go-vss Snapshotter does not remove the shadow copy: it is
created in the auto-release backup context, yet it outlives Release()
(which returns nil), often until the process exits. A GUI or service
stays running between backups, so each shadow lingered for hours, kept
copy-on-write going on the volume, and its shadow storage counted as
used space in the next machine backup (27.8 GB on a laptop's C:).

- The shadows a CreateVSSSnapshot call created are now deleted by ID
  once the backup callback is done, successful or not (before: only on
  the error path).
- The deletes go through IVssBackupComponents.DeleteSnapshots instead of
  vssadmin. vssadmin refuses backup-context shadows ("Snapshots were
  found, but they were outside of your allowed context"), so neither the
  error-path delete nor VSSCleanup's startup sweep ever removed one.
- go-vss numbers its VSS_CTX_* constants with iota, so its VSS_CTX_ALL
  (6) is rejected with VSS_E_UNSUPPORTED_CONTEXT; the real value,
  0xffffffff, is passed.
- go-ole becomes a direct dependency of the snapshot module.

Opt-in live tests in zz_vss_release_livetest_windows_test.go:
TestLiveVSSSnapshotReleased (PBS_VSS_VOL, elevated) checks the shadow is
gone when CreateVSSSnapshot returns; TestLiveVSSCleanup (PBS_VSS_CLEANUP=1)
runs the startup sweep. On Windows 10: before the change the shadow was
still listed 21 s after return; after, it is gone at once, and the sweep
removed 7 leftovers from earlier runs.

Fork: conflict with our in-use shadow check (VSSCleanup keeps a shadow another backup of ours is reading) resolved by keeping that check; stale vssadmin comments updated.

Upstream: tizbac/proxmoxbackupclient_go@630ed7b408a9dd02538be0f6136bc1bbd50791c7
